/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446569 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446569 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446569 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9999ac46-6f4e-593f-907b-51eaf6bed9c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446570 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b667cd59-08f8-5c6e-82c5-8b3ea70d6405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446571 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dfc4e87e-69ab-505e-8ac2-a813c8707da5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446572 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446572 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446572 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5a/5ac051f640c2b4eae760eee9c567ec35ce3169384ec2305554e21c6d2d587bbe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n\nIV. $F$ is irrotational" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446572 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "322bc9a1-81a0-53ce-920d-1108825e3484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446572 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/80/80c67e624f960147b7980ee90700be800a414ab1e2a2e47faf7e8821ee0c0623.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446573 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/18/183ea541b33b8389de46f9ee1560ed1da2b999191477d7490eb4970922bdc43c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $F$ is not irrotational\n\nIV. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40fa6adc-4679-5e13-a9fc-cb698f54e0e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446573 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1520791fcb3808604758b7d8f99443020d12075d4cc8bff78659880575b6ef2.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III and IV are false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational. And, since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446574 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446574 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446574 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446574 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(3x+2y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(-2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446574 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aedf76f0-5451-58bc-9366-057502daac94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446574 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x+2y) \\\\  & =-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-2,1)} & =-2(-2)-2 \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
